.TH ENGINE1999 1 "October 2026" "Engine 1999 @VERSION@" "Engine1999 Manual"
.SH NAME
engine1999 \- a retro home computer with a built\-in BASIC, in a window
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B engine1999
[\fIoption\fR ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B engine1999
opens a window that behaves like a home computer of the late 1980s and early 1990s: an 80x25 text screen, a tile layer, sprites, a free\-drawing
graphics layer and a software synthesizer, and a BASIC to program them.
It shows the
.B Ready.
prompt of the BASIC system.
A line that starts with a line number is stored in the program, any other line runs at once (direct mode).
.PP
Type a program and
.BR RUN
it, or load one:
.PP
.RS
.nf
LOAD "/usr/local/share/engine1999/demos/fruit2"
RUN
.fi
.RE
.PP
The language is described in
.BR engine1999\-basic (7).
A program can be turned into a standalone executable with the BASIC statement
.BR BUILD ;
see that page.
For scripts and servers without a window use
.BR engine1999c (1).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a short help text and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-debug\-tokenize
Print the token bytes of every input line (to standard output).
.TP
.B \-\-debug\-detokenize
Print every input line rebuilt from its tokens.
.TP
.B \-\-debug\-parse
Print every token as the compiler fetches it.
.TP
.B \-\-debug\-tree
Print the syntax tree after parsing.
.TP
.B \-\-debug\-code
Print every instruction the code generator emits.
.TP
.B \-\-debug\-all
All of the debug outputs (not
.BR \-\-debug\-keys ", " \-\-debug\-frames ).
.TP
.B \-\-debug\-frames
Print, for every frame in which the mouse did something (it moved, a button, the wheel), the times of the frame in microseconds: the period since the previous frame, when the events were polled, how long moving the pointer took, the redraw, the present (which waits for the vertical blank), the sleep, and the age of the oldest event until the frame is on the screen. For finding out why the pointer feels slow. Run it from a terminal or redirect the output to a file.
.TP
.B \-\-debug\-keys
Show what the keyboard delivers: every SDL key event (key code, name, scan code, modifiers) and every text input is printed to standard output, with the escape sequence the program would receive for the key (\fBESC [ 1 ; 2 A\fR for Shift+Up, see
.BR engine1999\-basic (7)).
Run it from a terminal, or redirect the output to a file.
.SH AT THE PROMPT
In the window the prompt is a screen editor, as on the Commodore 64: the text on the screen is the buffer.
Move the cursor anywhere (it moves freely over empty cells and scrolls the screen at the edges, nothing is kept off screen),
edit what is there and press RETURN: the line under the cursor (the text around it up to the next empty cell, wrapped rows included) is entered,
wherever it was typed or printed.
The bottom row is a status line (white on translucent dark red) that shows the typing mode, the row and column and the BASIC line number of the line;
the screen rows of the line are highlighted.
Typing overwrites at first;
.B F12
(or Shift+INSERT) switches between overwrite and insert mode.
.B INSERT
inserts a space,
.B DELETE
and
.B BACKSPACE
close the gap; BACKSPACE at the start of a line only moves the cursor left, it never deletes beyond the line.
HOME and END go to the start and end of the line (END on a line that fills its last row opens a new screen line below it);
Break (Ctrl+C, PAUSE) does nothing while no program runs; Ctrl+HOME and Ctrl+END to the top left and bottom left of the screen.
In a row between two gaps the keys work on the rest of the screen row (for ASCII graphics).
.B INPUT, AUTO
and
.B EDIT n
use the plain one\-line editor, and so does a program that is running.
.TP
.B ESC, Ctrl+C, PAUSE/BREAK
Stop a running program ("Break"), also one that waits for a key or a line.
A program can take ESC for itself with
.BR "BREAK OFF" ;
Ctrl+C and PAUSE/BREAK still stop it.
Closing the window ends the program and the Engine.
.TP
.B F11, ALT+RETURN
Switch the window between the full screen and a normal, resizable window (a whole multiple of the 640 x 300 screen, centered).
The Engine starts in the full screen. The title of the window is the name of the executable (a program made with
.B BUILD
shows its own name). The keys are used up by the Engine: a program does not receive them
.RB "(" KEY("F11")
still tells that F11 is held).
.TP
.B LIST, SAVE, LOAD, RUN, NEW, RENUM, DELETE, MERGE, CHAIN, FILES
Program commands; they are ordinary BASIC statements and work inside programs, too.
.TP
.B EDIT
Without a target: a full\-screen editor for the program (ESC leaves it). The mouse works in it: a click in the text puts the cursor there; the wheel and the scroll bar on the right scroll it (click or hold the arrows, click the track, drag the thumb: the pointer is a hand then).
.B EDIT 100
edits one line.
.TP
.B MONITOR
A machine monitor: disassembler, memory, single step and breakpoints of the program's virtual machine.
.TP
.B BYE [code]
.TQ
.B QUIT [code]
End the Engine; the process exits with the code (0 to 255, default 0).
.TP
.B SHOW WARRANTY
.TQ
.B SHOW COPYING
Show the terms of the license (the GNU General Public License and the Engine1999 Runtime Exception).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SDL_GAMECONTROLLERCONFIG
SDL's mapping strings for game controllers that SDL does not know (see the SDL Game Controller DB); a controller without a mapping is still usable through the plain joystick fallback
(see
.BR engine1999\-basic (7),
JOY).
.TP
.B SDL_VIDEO_FULLSCREEN_DISPLAY
The number of the display on which the window opens. Without it the window opens on the display that holds the mouse pointer.
.TP
.BR SDL_VIDEODRIVER ", " SDL_AUDIODRIVER
Chosen as for any SDL2 program (for example
.B SDL_AUDIODRIVER=disk
to write the sound to a file).
.TP
.B ENGINE1999_HOME
The directory with the runtime kit (\fIengine1999.o\fR, \fIengine1999c.o\fR, \fIengine.lib\fR) that
.B BUILD
uses; see
.BR engine1999\-basic (7).
.TP
.B ENGINE1999_LD
The linker that
.B BUILD
uses (default: the first
.BR ld (1)
in the PATH).
.TP
.B ENGINE1999_BUILD_TRACE
If set to something other than 0,
.B BUILD
prints the commands it runs to standard error.
.TP
.BR LIBRARY_PATH ", " TMPDIR
Extra library directories for
.BR BUILD ;
the directory for its temporary files.
.PP
A program can read any variable with the BASIC function
.BR ENV$ .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I *.bas
Programs as text listings (what
.B SAVE "name"
writes).
.TP
.I *.e99
Programs in the binary tokenized form (what
.B SAVE "name",B
writes).
.B LOAD "name"
finds either.
.TP
.I res/
The directory read by the BASIC driver
.BR res: ,
below the current directory.
.TP
.I /usr/local/lib/engine1999/
The runtime kit that
.B BUILD
needs, and
.TP
.I /usr/local/share/engine1999/demos/
the demo programs, after
.BR "make install" .
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when the Engine ends normally, the code of
.B BYE
or
.BR QUIT ,
2 for a bad command line, 1 if the display can't be opened.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR engine1999c (1),
.BR engine1999\-basic (7),
.BR engine1999\-fruit (6),
.BR engine1999\-fruit2 (6),
.BR engine1999\-climb (6),
.BR engine1999\-madrun (6),
.BR engine1999\-river (6)
.PP
The full documentation is in /usr/local/share/doc/engine1999/ (docs/ in the source tree); the whole syntax is on one page in grammar.html there.
